  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nThis afternoon through next Friday...\n\nCurrent radar scans show scattered showers and storms tracking west \nacross Hwy 84. This is due to an easterly wave bringing a renewed \nsurge of moisture mainly across portions of southeast MS. Rain \nchances will increase later this afternoon with the best chances for \nscattered showers and storms mainly along and south of I-20. A few \nstrong to marginally severe storms cannot be ruled out. Hot and \nhumid conditions will persist across much of the area today with \nafternoon highs peaking in the mid to upper 90s and heat index \nreading values peaking around 105-110 degrees. A few spots across \nportions of central MS could see heat index values briefly exceed \n110 degrees this afternoon. No changes have been made to the heat \ngraphic for today and an \"Elevated\" risk for dangerous heat will \ncontinue to be advertised for much of the area. Likewise, the heat \nadvisory remains in effect for parts of the area, including portions \nof southeast AR and all of northeast LA, until 7 pm this evening. \n\nRain chances will begin to decrease across much of the area later \nthis evening, with a few lingering showers and storms possible \nmainly along and south of Hwy 84. Quiet conditions will prevail \nacross our entire forecast area by midnight with overnight lows \ndipping into the low 70s. As we head into early Saturday morning, \nclouds will begin to increase in coverage from the south to the \nnorth. Although HREF fog guidance is beginning to show low \nprobabilities (around 10-20%) of patchy fog across our entire \nforecast area, there is not enough support from the REFS to suggest \nwidespread fog potential. Fog graphics will not be introduced at \nthis time given the low probability of fog development highlighted \nby HREF dense fog guidance, however shallow patchy fog will be \npossible particularly after dawn. \n\nUpper level ridging will continue to dominate the regional weather, \nthough will shift from the Mid-South to the High Plains and then \nback again over the next several days. Limited to elevated heat \nstress will continue to be the primary focus for the long term as \nheat index values are forecasted to peak in the 105-110 degree range \nacross much of the area through the Labor Day Weekend. Late \nafternoon/early evening showers and storms should help keep parts of \nour CWA under Heat Advisory criteria. With the deep tropical \nmoisture in place, some of these storms will have the capability to \nproduce locally heavy downpours at times. Regardless, an Heat \nAdvisory has been issued for much of our CWA for Saturday. As we go \nbeyond Saturday, additional heat advisories will eventually be \nneeded for Sunday and Monday. There is a possibility that an Extreme \nHeat Warning could be introduced for portions of our forecast area \non Sunday. Heat trends will continue to be monitored as we go \nthrough the weekend. \n\nDangerous heat will continue to persist across the area as we head \ninto the new work week. Additional heat headlines/advisories will \neventually be needed as we get closer. Heat trends will begin to \ndecrease across parts of the area by the mid week as global guidance \nshows a frontal boundary pushing south across the area. Rain chances \nwill be possible mainly for areas along and south of I-20 heading \ninto Thursday with higher chances across much of the area on \nFriday. /CR/",
